     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "car_list = [\"KIA\", \"Ferrari\", \"Ford\", \"Tesla\"]\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"Dear customer, we are writing about your \" + car_list[0] + \" vehicle.\")\n",
    "print(\"Dear customer, we are writing about your \" + car_list[1] + \" vehicle.\")\n",
    "print(\"Dear customer, we are writing about your \" + car_list[2] + \" vehicle.\")\n",
    "print(\"Dear customer, we are writing about your \" + car_list[3] + \" vehicle.\")\n",
    "\n",
    "# Here we're concatenating strings with a \"+\" sign"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "3f6480cb",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "<font size = \"4\">\n",
    "\n",
    "Now, with a **for loop**"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 6,
   "id": "0c50dfb6",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "del car_list\n",
    "\n",
    "# del car_name"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 7,
   "id": "1749473b",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Dear customer, we are writing about your KIA vehicle.\n",
      "Dear customer, we are writing about your Ferrari vehicle.\n",
      "Dear customer, we are writing about your Ford vehicle.\n",
      "Dear customer, we are writing about your Tesla vehicle.\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "# Now, with a for loop\n",
    "\n",
    "car_list = [\"KIA\", \"Ferrari\", \"Ford\", \"Tesla\"]\n",
    "\n",
    "car_name = \"Tesla\"\n",
    "\n",
    "for car_name in car_list:\n",
    "    print(\"Dear customer, we are writing about your \" + car_name + \" vehicle.\")\n",
    "\n",
    "# The code \"for car_name in car_list:\", means... \n",
    "\n",
    "# A variable \"car_name\" takes the value of every item in the list \"car_list\", one by one\n",
    "# For each value of car_name, the indented block of code is executed once."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "75e90c1f",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "<font size = \"4\">\n",
    "\n",
    "This next code cell is **exactly** the same as the one above.\n",
    "\n",
    "We are \"unrolling\" the loop, so that every instruction is specified individually"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "03f69004",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "car_list = [\"KIA\", \"Ferrari\", \"Ford\", \"Tesla\"]\n",
    "\n",
    "# car_name is the \"loop variable\"\n",
    "car_name = \"KIA\"\n",
    "print(\"Dear customer, we are writing about your \" + car_name + \" vehicle.\")\n",
    "\n",
    "car_name = \"Ferrari\"\n",
    "print(\"Dear customer, we are writing about your \" + car_name + \" vehicle.\")\n",
    "\n",
    "car_name = \"Ford\"\n",
    "print(\"Dear customer, we are writing about your \" + car_name + \" vehicle.\")\n",
    "\n",
    "car_name = \"Tesla\"\n",
    "print(\"Dear customer, we are writing about your \" + car_name + \" vehicle.\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "ca8f5cbb",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "<font size = \"4\">\n",
    "\n",
    "When writing a for loop, it's often a good idea to start with an \"unrolled\" version, like the cell above. Once you understand the structure, you can convert it to a for loop with some copy-and-pasting:"
   ]
